# Under --parallel, a foreground prompt with little left to fill goes before the next decode round (TF_GLM_QUICK_ROWS):
#
# - Why: while other streams decode, prompt chunks take only TF_GLM_FILL_SHARE of the iterations (default 0.5: a chunk, a
#   round, a chunk, ...). A burst of short chat prompts therefore reached its first tokens one chunk per round, the
#   streams already decoding taking a round between every two. A prompt that one chunk finishes costs the decoding streams
#   one chunk and gives its client the first token at once.
# - multi.quick_rows / MultiDecoder.quick: TF_GLM_QUICK_ROWS (default 1,024, at most the TF_GLM_FILL_ROWS chunk, so one
#   chunk; 0 = off; anything else but a count is an error). MultiDecoder._quick(lane): a foreground stream with at most
#   that many prompt rows left (a replay of a kept head: none). _fill_turn: with streams decoding and no sliced fill
#   (TF_GLM_FILL_BUDGET_MS=0), the oldest quick foreground filling stream (next_fill) fills without taking the share's
#   turn (credit untouched); otherwise the share's turn as before. Background prompts never jump the queue; nothing
#   changes with no stream decoding, with sliced fills, or with TF_GLM_MULTI_PREFILL's grouping (the quick stream is
#   the group's first piece).
# - Rank 0 decides; rank 1 applies the FILL / MFILL it is sent, so no setting needs to agree between ranks.
#
# Bits: only the order of whole prompt chunks and decode rounds between streams changes. Each stream's chunks are cut at
# the same rows (_stop is untouched) and each stream's rows and tokens depend on its own state alone, so replies and
# committed caches equal those with TF_GLM_QUICK_ROWS=0 (and each request served alone).
#
# Tests (CPU, tests/cpu/test_multi_quick_fills.py in the recipe: the real _fill_turn / _iterate over stand-in forwards):
# the setting, a burst's order of chunks and rounds on and off, a long prompt alternating until its rest is quick,
# background prompts never quick, the oldest quick prompt first, an idle server unchanged, replies and every stream's
# chunk boundaries equal on and off at three shares, and short prompts' first tokens sooner.
#
# Provenance and credit: ported from the GLM-5.3 recipe's patch 0167 (Mia's AI Lab). The quick fills idea is
# bertholomus' (BertholomusAI, Albert Lee): TF_GLM_QUICK_ROWS in github.com/bertholomus/TensorFold, branch glm-dsa-tp4,
# commit 14b43b0, Apache 2.0. The scheduler code here is ours; none of theirs.
